  "commit": "6ee9b68792ce6130f430aaf915051a4d2f44a697",
  "description": "THE ITERATION AN ADVERSARIAL CONSULTATION PAID FOR. run/0021 closed on its own ledger pass; this run is everything that followed from asking a Fable agent to attack the belief that the remaining v1 work is entirely owner-gated. Four of its findings were verified against the tree first-hand before anything was touched, and three of the four were worse than the report said.\n\nWHAT THE MERGES ARE. #374 rewrote the operations runbook's Trash section, which told an operator the opposite of the code on the one irreversible operation in the product: trash purge refuses BECAUSE a period is archived, where period purge REQUIRES it, there is no retention window at that door at all, and the directory purge the page described does not exist. #376 gave Run.merges a field pair per main-push workflow and a guard that refuses if the set drifts. #378 deleted a directory literally named `undefined` that had been shipping 16,681 unreferenced bytes into every image layer, and made the class refuse. #379 corrected four texts the tree contradicts, two of them worse than reported.\n\nTHE RUN BOUNDARY IS ITSELF A FINDING, recorded in run/0021's closing note: nothing triggers it, so it drifts open until somebody decides. This run is opened deliberately rather than inherited, and startTime is the loop trigger's own firing time (2026-09-21T20:44:58Z) rather than a round number -- 23 seconds after run/0021's close, which is also what makes it strictly after, as the validator requires.\n\nMY OWN TWO MISTAKES THIS RUN, both caught by CI on #376 and both worth the space. I inserted a constant BETWEEN checkMergeEntry()'s docblock and its signature, orphaning the `@param list<string>` and costing phpstan the by-ref type at the call site. And I rewrote two blocks that .github/mutations.json registers by exact source text, which mutation-check caught and named precisely. The second was repointed, not weakened: same expectFailing tests, and each new `remove` text was applied to a copy of the validator and shown to silence it -- because \"I updated the registry to match\" is the sentence a weakened guard would also produce.\n\nTHE CLAUSE SWEEP LOOP STEP 4 MANDATES, RUN IN FULL AND REPORTED EMPTY. Every clause of every open v1 item -- 682 lines across six items, eligible or not -- read rather than summarised, because the item-granularity reading has been wrong seven times in this project and the previous iteration reported \"all owner-gated\" at exactly that granularity. It holds at clause granularity too: item/release-v0-1-0's three proofs, item/image-size's \"printed by the release job\", item/readme-owner's clause 2, item/v1-audit's clause (c) subject and item/tag-v1-0-0's clauses (1)(2)(3) all need one tag push; item/docs-site needs two repository settings. Nothing buildable was found, which is what makes a ledger-only pull request permitted here.\n\nTHE SWEEP PAID FOR ITSELF ANYWAY, twice. item/tag-v1-0-0's clause (1) records that mutation/0057 exists to prove widening assert-release-gate.py's edge list did not weaken the edge decision/0120 put there -- and PR #366 widened it again, with two more edges and a gate-equality rule, without re-running that mutant. Re-run here: dropping boot-published-image still exits 1, and so does dropping image -> changelog-section. The guarantee survives, now checked rather than inherited. And the sweep turned up that every release.yml line citation in the ledger had rotted, which is this pass's other half.\n\nTHE COMPOSE STACK BOOTED FOR THE FIRST TIME, 2026-09-22. Every `docker compose` invocation CI had ever run was `config`, which renders and interpolates the file and starts nothing -- so compose-secret proved the secret is required and proved nothing about whether the stack runs, while CLAUDE.md calls `docker compose up -d --build` \"the real stack\" and quick-start.md offers it as a first-class install path.\n\nI ALSO OVER-BLOCKED MYSELF ON IT, one iteration earlier, and the correction is the reusable part. I reported the whole item blocked on issue #382, having conflated what the DOCS should say (the owner's) with what CI should ASSERT (mine). Booting the default stack and asserting the app answers needs no product decision at all; only the sibling-MinIO half does. That is LOOP step 6's standing lesson one level up -- a refused command establishes that THAT command was refused, and an open decision establishes that THAT decision is open, not that everything touching it is blocked.",
